        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. 1. The Short Answer: Not at Any Level You Can Live With
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ozone generators are sold with a simple promise: a gas that spreads into every corner, reaches places a filter cannot, kills mold and erases the musty smell. Ozone is a genuinely strong oxidiser, so the pitch is not invented from nothing. The problem is concentration. The EPA's review of ozone generators sold as air cleaners concludes that ozone at levels that do not exceed public health standards has little effect on mold and other biological contaminants, and that to be effective it would have to be several times higher than those standards allow.
          </p>

          <p>
            That splits the product into two uses, neither of which fixes mold in a lived-in home. A small generator running at a level that is safe to breathe does essentially nothing to the mold. A large generator running at a level that could affect mold makes the space unsafe for anyone inside, which is why the products intended for it are sold for 'shock treatment' of empty rooms, vehicles and buildings, with instructions to seal the space and stay out until the ozone has dissipated.
          </p>

          <p>
            Even the high-concentration use falls short as a mold treatment. Ozone reacts with the first thing it touches, so it is consumed by surfaces, dust and furnishings before it reaches growth tucked into wall cavities, carpet backing or the paper face of drywall. The EPA notes that ozone may have no effect on biological contaminants embedded in porous material. And whatever ozone does kill stays where it is, still able to trigger allergies, while the moisture that fed the colony is still there to grow the next one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Ozone does not remediate mold. Removal and drying do.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">At breathable levels ozone does little to mold. At effective levels the space must be empty, materials are damaged, and hidden growth is untouched. Dead spores remain allergenic. Professional standards rely on physical removal, not gas.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you have read this far because a musty room is bothering you, the useful question is what will actually lower the spores and odor you are breathing. That is a true HEPA air purifier, with a heavy carbon filter for the smell, alongside a fix for the moisture. Our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best air purifiers for mold</a> are ranked on exactly those criteria, and none of them relies on ozone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-ozone-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. 2. How Ozone Attacks Mold, and Why Surfaces Protect It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ozone is a molecule of three oxygen atoms. It is unstable and eager to give up the third atom, which makes it highly reactive with organic material. When it reaches a microbe, it oxidises the cell wall and membrane, and with enough exposure it damages the contents. In water treatment, where ozone is dissolved and mixed thoroughly with the water for a controlled time, it is an effective disinfectant. The difference in a house is that ozone is a gas spreading through a room full of other things it would rather react with.
          </p>

          <p>
            That reactivity is the problem. Indoors, ozone reacts with carpets, upholstery, painted walls, wood, skin oils, dust and the vapours from cleaning products and air fresheners. Each reaction uses up ozone, so the concentration at a moldy surface is lower than the concentration at the generator, and much lower inside a wall or under a floor. The same chemistry is why ozone levels fall fairly quickly once a generator is switched off: it is being consumed by the room.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mold growth adds its own protection. A colony is not a single layer of spores on a clean surface. It is a mat of hyphae growing into the material, often mixed with dust and the material's own fibres. Ozone that does reach it reacts with the outer layers first. Laboratory studies that report useful kills of fungi with gaseous ozone typically use clean test surfaces, controlled humidity and concentrations well into the parts per million held for hours. A real moldy basement wall, with growth inside the drywall and behind it, is a much harder target.
          </p>

          <p>
            Moisture matters too. Some studies find ozone is more effective against microbes at high relative humidity, which is awkward, because a room at high humidity is exactly the kind of room that will regrow mold as soon as the treatment ends. There is no ozone setting that fixes a damp room. That alone rules ozone out as a primary treatment: even if it worked perfectly on the day, the conditions that produced the mold are unchanged the next day.
          </p>

          <p>
            So when you see a claim that an ozone generator 'kills 99 percent of mold', ask what was tested. Was it spores on a clean glass slide in a sealed chamber, at what concentration, for how long? Those conditions say little about a closet with mold behind the baseboard. The honest summary is that ozone can damage mold that it reaches at high concentration, and that in a home it reliably reaches only the easy parts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ductwork is a good example of the gap. Sellers sometimes suggest running a generator into the return to treat mold in the ducts. The EPA specifically notes that ozone may have no effect on biological contaminants embedded in porous material such as duct lining. Flex duct liners, duct board and dusty sheet metal all consume ozone at the surface and shelter what lies beneath. Contaminated duct board and liner is normally replaced, and the cause, usually condensation on uninsulated or leaky ducts, corrected.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-numbers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. 3. The Numbers: Concentrations Needed vs Safe Limits
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ozone in air is measured in parts per million, and the gap between what is safe and what does anything to mold is the whole story. The FDA limits ozone output from indoor medical devices to no more than 0.05 parts per million. OSHA's workplace limit is 0.10 parts per million averaged over an eight-hour shift, and NIOSH recommends that 0.10 parts per million not be exceeded at any time. California's air cleaner regulation caps emissions from certified indoor air cleaning devices at 0.050 parts per million.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Reference point</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ozone level</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What it means</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">CARB limit for certified indoor air cleaners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.050 ppm emission concentration</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Air cleaners sold in California must stay under this</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">FDA limit for indoor medical devices</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.05 ppm</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Maximum output for a device used in occupied rooms</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">OSHA workplace limit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.10 ppm, 8-hour average</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Legal exposure limit for workers</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">NIOSH recommended ceiling</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.10 ppm, never exceeded</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Recommended upper bound at any moment</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">What the EPA says is needed to decontaminate air</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 to 10 times public health standards</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Above safe limits for any occupied space</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">NIOSH immediately dangerous to life or health</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 ppm</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The level at which escape-only protection is required</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Against those limits, the EPA states that ozone concentrations would have to be 5 to 10 times higher than public health standards allow before ozone could decontaminate the air, and that killing contaminants on surfaces generally needs more. Surface studies that report meaningful mold kills commonly use whole parts per million, held for hours. That is tens of times above the FDA and CARB figures, and it puts high-output shock treatments in the same range as the 5 parts per million NIOSH lists as immediately dangerous to life or health.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is also why the small 'ionic' or 'activated oxygen' units sold for bedrooms are in an awkward position. If they emit little ozone, they do little to mold. If they emit a lot, they create a breathing hazard in the room they are meant to improve. Some of these devices have been measured producing indoor concentrations above the public health limits, particularly in small, closed rooms. Manufacturers rarely publish the concentration their unit produces in a real room, only an output rating in milligrams per hour, which does not tell you what you will breathe.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Gap in Four Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0.05 ppm</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">FDA limit for indoor medical devices</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0.10 ppm</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">OSHA 8-hour workplace limit</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">5-10x</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Over health standards to clean air, per EPA</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">5 ppm</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">NIOSH immediately dangerous level</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The practical takeaway is simple. There is no concentration of ozone that is both safe to breathe and effective against mold. Any product claiming both, whether it is called an ozone generator, an activated oxygen purifier or a 'super oxygen' cleaner, is promising something the chemistry does not allow. Our sister explainer on <a href="/does-uv-light-kill-mold/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">whether UV light kills mold</a> reaches a similar conclusion about another kill-it-in-the-air technology, for different physical reasons.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-pros-do" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. 4. What Remediation Pros Actually Use Ozone For
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ozone does have a professional role, and it is narrower than the consumer marketing suggests. Restoration contractors use high-output generators for odor control, most often after fire and smoke damage, and sometimes after a mold job or in vehicles, rental units and buildings with persistent smells. The work is done as a 'shock treatment': the space is sealed, every person, pet and plant is removed, the generator runs for a set period, and nobody re-enters until the space has been aired and ozone has fallen back to safe levels, which often takes several hours or more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Crucially, ozone in professional mold work comes after the mold is gone, not instead of removing it. The IICRC S520 standard for professional mold remediation is built around source removal: identifying and correcting the moisture source, containing the work area, physically removing contaminated material, HEPA vacuuming and damp wiping what remains, and verifying the result. Gas treatments, fogging and coatings are not accepted substitutes for that. A contractor who proposes to 'ozone the house' without finding the water and removing the growth is a red flag.
          </p>

          <p>
            When ozone is used after remediation, the goal is residual odor in materials that were cleaned but not removed, such as framing that has been HEPA vacuumed and wiped. Even then, many contractors prefer hydroxyl generators or simply time, ventilation and activated carbon for odor, because ozone damages materials and can leave its own sharp smell. If you are paying for ozone, it should be a final odor step in an unoccupied building, with a documented re-entry time, not the treatment itself.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">IF A CONTRACTOR OFFERS OZONE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Ask what is being removed first.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A legitimate plan names the moisture source, the containment, what material comes out, and how the result will be checked. Ozone, if it appears at all, is a last odor step in an empty building. A plan that is mostly ozone is not remediation.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            For homeowners, renting or buying a shock generator to treat your own house is the use most likely to go wrong. People underestimate re-entry times, leave pets or plants inside, run generators in rooms with gas appliances and rubber seals, or treat an occupied apartment next door through shared walls and ducts. If the mold area is small, generally around 10 square feet or less by the EPA's long-standing guidance, clean it yourself with detergent and water and proper protection. If it is larger, hire a remediator who follows S520.
          </p>

          <p>
            Professional jobs also end with verification rather than a smell test. That typically means a visual inspection showing no remaining growth or dust, moisture readings confirming materials are dry, and in many cases air or surface sampling compared against an outdoor baseline. Ozone has no place in that checklist, because it changes nothing a sample would measure except, briefly, the odor. If your contractor cannot tell you how success will be checked, the job is not finished when they leave.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dead-mold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. 5. Dead Mold Is Still a Problem: Allergens, Mycotoxins and Regrowth
          </h2>

          <p>
            Suppose ozone did kill every spore in a room. The spores would still be there, on surfaces, in carpet, in dust and in the air. Mold allergens are proteins and cell wall components, and they do not need a living spore to trigger a reaction. The EPA notes the same point about UV cleaners: dead mold spores can still produce allergic reactions. For anyone whose reason for treating mold is sneezing, congestion or asthma, a dead-but-present colony is not much of an improvement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some molds also produce mycotoxins, chemical compounds that are not alive and therefore cannot be 'killed'. Ozone can oxidise some of them in laboratory conditions, but it cannot be relied on to destroy toxins bound up in building materials and dust. Professional guidance treats mold as a material to be removed for this reason. Growth that has been killed and left in place is still contaminated material.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ozone also tends to mask rather than solve the musty smell. Mold odor comes from microbial volatile organic compounds, gases released by growing colonies. Ozone oxidises some of them and adds its own sharp, chlorine-like smell, so a room can smell 'fresh' immediately after treatment. If the colony is still alive somewhere ozone did not reach, or regrows because the moisture is unchanged, the musty smell returns within days or weeks. That pattern is one of the most common signs a treatment addressed the symptom rather than the cause.
          </p>

          <p>
            Regrowth is the deeper issue. Mold returns wherever moisture returns: a leak, condensation on cold surfaces, or relative humidity that stays high for long periods. Keeping indoor humidity at or below about 50 percent is what stops new growth. Ozone does nothing to humidity, and in a basement or bathroom it leaves the conditions for regrowth exactly as they were. For basements in particular, our guide on <a href="/how-to-get-rid-of-mold-in-basement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">how to get rid of mold in a basement</a> covers drying the space and keeping it dry, which is the step ozone skips.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="side-effects" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. 6. What Ozone Does to Your Lungs and Your House
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ozone is a respiratory irritant. Breathing it can cause coughing, chest pain, shortness of breath and throat irritation, and it can worsen asthma and reduce the lungs' ability to fight infection. People with asthma, children, older adults and people who are active indoors are the most sensitive. That is a particularly poor trade when the reason for buying an ozone generator is a household member who is already reacting to mold. The same lungs that are irritated by spores are now being irritated by the treatment.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ozone also creates new pollutants. It reacts with chemicals common indoors, such as the terpenes in pine and citrus scented cleaners and air fresheners, to form formaldehyde, other aldehydes, organic acids and ultrafine particles. The EPA lists aldehydes and formic acid among these byproducts. In other words, a room filled with ozone can end up with a different and arguably worse set of irritants in the air than it started with.
          </p>

          <p>
            Materials suffer too. The EPA notes that ozone can damage rubber, electrical wire coatings and fabrics, and it can harm indoor plants. In practice that means cracked rubber seals and gaskets on appliances and doors, faded or weakened fabrics and dyes, and degraded elastic and foam. Artwork, photographs and some finishes are also vulnerable. Shock treatments at high concentrations accelerate all of this, which is one reason professionals remove sensitive items before treatment and limit the duration.
          </p>

          <p>
            California's regulation exists because of these effects. Since October 2010, indoor air cleaning devices sold in or shipped to California must be certified to emit ozone at a concentration under 0.050 parts per million, and CARB maintains a list of certified devices as well as warnings about ozone generators sold as air purifiers. Devices designed to produce ozone intentionally cannot meet that limit and cannot be sold there as air cleaners. Wherever you live, the CARB certified list is a fast way to screen a product before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spotting an ozone device is not always easy, because few are labelled that way. Watch for terms such as activated oxygen, energised oxygen, super oxygen or trioxygen, for an output rating in milligrams per hour, and for corona discharge plates or ozone-producing UV lamps in the specifications. A sharp, chlorine-like smell near the unit after it runs is another giveaway. Any of those on a product sold for mold is a reason to keep shopping.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-do-instead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. 7. What to Do Instead of Running an Ozone Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the water. Find the leak, the condensation or the high humidity that let mold grow, and fix it. Use a hygrometer to check relative humidity, and if it sits above about 50 percent for long stretches, run a dehumidifier sized for the space. In a basement that is often the single most effective anti-mold step available. Our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best dehumidifiers for basements</a> guide covers the machines, and the <a href="/air-purifier-vs-dehumidifier/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">air purifier vs dehumidifier</a> comparison explains when you need one, the other or both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, remove the growth. Hard surfaces can be scrubbed with detergent and water and dried thoroughly. Porous materials such as drywall, ceiling tile and carpet pad that are badly colonised should be bagged and replaced. Wear an N95 respirator, gloves and eye protection, mist the surface lightly before disturbing it to keep spores down, and close the room off while you work. Above roughly 10 square feet of growth, or where sewage or contaminated water was involved, bring in a professional.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then clean the air. A true HEPA air purifier captures at least 99.97 percent of particles at 0.3 microns, and mold spores are larger than that, so it removes airborne spores and fragments without any of ozone's side effects. Run it on high during and after cleanup and continuously afterwards in a mold-prone room, sized for about five air changes an hour. Our explainer on <a href="/do-air-purifiers-help-with-mold/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">whether air purifiers help with mold</a> covers sizing, run time and placement in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the musty smell, use activated carbon, not ozone. Carbon adsorbs the volatile compounds that make up mold odor, and a purifier with a substantial carbon bed will steadily pull the smell down once the source is removed and the space is dry. Expect the carbon to saturate faster than its label suggests in a musty room. Washable items can be laundered, and a few weeks of dry air and fresh carbon usually does what an ozone shock only appears to do overnight.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, be wary of anything that promises to kill mold in the air: ozone generators, 'activated oxygen' purifiers, ionizers sold primarily for mold, and UV-only sanitizers. All of them aim at the airborne symptom rather than the growing colony, and the ozone-based ones carry real health costs. Removal, drying and filtration are less exciting, but they are what works, and they are what our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">mold purifier picks</a> are built around.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running an Ozone Generator in an Occupied Home</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ozone at levels that affect mold irritates lungs and worsens asthma, and the people bothered by mold are the ones most likely to react. Never run a generator where people, pets or plants are present. At breathable levels it does little to mold anyway, so there is no safe middle setting that makes it worthwhile.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating Mold Without Fixing Moisture</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ozone does nothing to humidity, leaks or condensation, so any mold it does damage regrows as soon as conditions allow. Find the water first and bring relative humidity to about 50 percent or below. Without that step, every treatment, whether ozone, chemical or physical, is temporary and the musty smell will return.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Believing a Fresh Smell Means the Mold Is Gone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ozone oxidises some odor compounds and leaves its own sharp smell, so a room can seem clean right after treatment. If live growth remains behind a wall or under flooring, the musty smell returns in days or weeks. A returning odor is a strong sign there is hidden growth still to find and remove.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Re-entering Too Soon After a Shock Treatment</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                High-output generators can push indoor ozone far above safe limits. Follow the manufacturer's re-entry time, air the space thoroughly before going back in, and remove pets, plants and sensitive items before starting. If you cannot seal the area from neighbours or shared ducts, do not run a shock treatment at all.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying 'Activated Oxygen' Purifiers for Mold</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Activated oxygen is a marketing name for ozone. These units either emit too little to affect mold or enough to irritate your lungs. Check any air cleaner against CARB's certified list, which caps emissions at 0.050 parts per million, and buy a true HEPA purifier with a published clean air delivery rate instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do ozone generators kill mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in any way that is useful in a lived-in home. The EPA says ozone at levels that meet public health standards has little effect on mold, and that it would need to be several times higher to decontaminate air. Even at high concentrations in empty rooms, ozone does not reach growth inside porous materials, and dead spores remain allergenic.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What concentration of ozone kills mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Studies that report meaningful mold kills on surfaces generally use concentrations of whole parts per million held for hours, which is tens of times above the FDA's 0.05 parts per million limit for medical devices and OSHA's 0.10 parts per million workplace limit. Those levels are only usable in sealed, unoccupied spaces and still do not remove the mold.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to run an ozone generator for mold while I am home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Ozone irritates the lungs, causing coughing, chest tightness and shortness of breath, and it worsens asthma. Generators powerful enough to affect mold create unsafe concentrations for anyone inside. If a generator is used at all, the space must be empty of people, pets and plants, and properly aired before re-entry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do mold remediation companies use ozone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some use it for odor control after the mold has been physically removed, usually in sealed, unoccupied buildings. The IICRC S520 standard for professional mold remediation relies on correcting moisture and removing contaminated material, not on gas treatments. Be cautious of any contractor whose main proposal is to ozone the house without removing growth.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does ozone get rid of musty smell?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can temporarily reduce it by oxidising some odor compounds, while adding its own sharp smell. If live mold remains or moisture persists, the musty smell returns. Removing the growth, drying the space and running a purifier with a substantial activated carbon filter is the more reliable and safer way to clear mold odor for good.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can ozone damage my home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. The EPA notes ozone can damage rubber, electrical wire coatings and fabrics, and harm indoor plants. High-concentration treatments can crack seals and gaskets, fade dyes and degrade foam and elastic. Ozone also reacts with chemicals from cleaners and air fresheners to form formaldehyde and other irritants, which can worsen indoor air.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are ozone air purifiers legal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In California, indoor air cleaning devices must be certified to emit ozone under 0.050 parts per million, so devices designed to produce ozone cannot be sold there as air cleaners. Elsewhere in the US they are widely available, but CARB's certified list is still a useful way to screen any purifier. Choose true HEPA with a published CADR instead.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

